the search for sunlight is long and  winding quite literally in fact for the  cucumber plant as climbing plants they  make use of tendrils specialized thread  like extensions that swing around in  search of a structure to latch on to one  stared in place a tendril pulls the rest  of the plant up behind it historically  the study of plant tendrils have been  documented since the 18th century  notably Darwin described tendril coils  as a series of soft Springs since then  we have come a long way in describing  their anatomy and mechanics it is known  for instance that the suspended portion  of a tendril coils into a helical shape  with at least two oppositely handed  helix EES connected by a helical  perversion this helical coiling shortens  the tendril lengthwise hoisting the  plant toward the attachment point our  recent findings have determined even  more mechanical and mathematical  specifics of tendril coiling this  coiling is made possible by a stiff  ribbon like strip of specialized cells  within tendrils called the fibre ribbon  during coiling the fibre ribbon  contracts asymmetrically when the  tendril is ready to coil a chemical  compound called lignin accumulates in  the ventral side of the ribbon this  stiffens the ventral side shortening it  relative to the dorsal side an  artificial model of this process is  depicted here it unwinds and flattens as  it is pulled from both ends to create  the model start with an unstrained  silicone rubber strip and stretch it  axially apply a silicone caulk layer and  allow it to cure it represents the most  rudimentary of models emulating nature  just some  but not completely here is a natural  tendril acting in much the same way as  the previous model as it is extended it  unwinds to the point of flattening out  now here is a natural tendril over  winding as it is extended so what causes  this difference let's turn to a more  complicated model here we can observe a  silicon model that has been adjusted to  over wined instead of unwind upon  stretching this adjustment has been made  by adding a copper wire to the exterior  of its helix and a fabric ribbon to its  interior together the fabric ribbon and  wire increased the models effective  bending stiffness with respect to  twisting stiffness making the intrinsic  helix curvature harder to change while  still allowing the model to twist around  its center line in other words the model  is less able to flatten out and more  likely to add coils to compensate for  the force being exerted on it from both  ends  mathematically if the force needed to  bend the material is less than the force  required to twist it there is more  bending than twisting and so the tendril  unwinds on extension flattening out  young tendrils tend to unwind as they  are typically easier to bend than to  twist  in contrast old tendrils tend to be  harder to bend than to twist and they  over wind when pulled but what does over  winding mean for the plant pulling leads  to a two phase response by the tendril  first it acts like a soft spring while  over winding but then it enters a second  more resistant phase as it begins to  unwind this unusual type of spring with  a two phase mechanical response has  never been seen before in nature or in  engineering design our investigations of  humber tendrils provide insight into how  these plants reach a vital resource  sunlight and furthermore suggest a new  design for Springs that may prove useful  in engineering
 

